        "faq_YPHreL": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will they fit under nitrile gloves?",
            "answer": "Yes. DRYE is 0.4mm thin and fits under nitrile, latex, vinyl, rubber and many sealed work gloves without changing the feel much.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_wCzg8J": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I wear them under my gloves?",
            "answer": "Yes. DRYE goes on first, then your work glove. That’s how the moisture-routing layer works.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_8BwAyY": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will my hands get hotter?",
            "answer": "Any extra layer can add warmth. But inside a sealed glove, humidity can reach 90% within 5–7 minutes and skin temperature can climb to 35°C / 95°F.\n\nThat trapped sweat often feels hotter than DRYE’s thin 0.4 mm textile layer. Most users describe a drier, more breathable, and more comfortable glove feel — and don’t want to go back to skin-to-glove contact.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_FeFy9M": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if oil gets on them?",
            "answer": "Unlike cotton, DRYE doesn’t soak up oil. Wipe off surface grease after the shift and machine wash when needed.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_RmnBHQ": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why put anything under nitrile?",
            "answer": "Because changing gloves doesn’t fix the wet layer inside them. Cotton soaks. Creams come too late. DRYE adds a thin dry layer that moves sweat away from your skin while you work.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_cVYHKP": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will they feel weird?",
            "answer": "New, not weird. They’re 0.4mm thin, so most users adjust after a wear or two. The first time you pull your gloves off to drier hands, the extra step makes sense.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_fKYhkJ": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long do they last? / Can I wash them?",
            "answer": "With heavy daily use, one pair typically lasts around 6 months. Machine wash up to 90°C / 194°F. Air dry or tumble dry. No coatings or membranes to wash out.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_aqPJyd": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is this just cotton?",
            "answer": "No. Cotton absorbs sweat and stays wet. DRYE doesn’t absorb on the skin side — moisture moves outward so the skin-side stays dry.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_ae8QL9": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "I get eczema or cracked hands from gloves. Is DRYE worth it?",
            "answer": "Yes — this is the main reason why most of our customers (mechanics, tradespeople, and other glove users) buy DRYE. Many report significantly healthier hands after using the liners under their nitrile gloves.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_mKhyiL": {
          "type": "faq",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is nitrile and latex allergenic to my skin?",
            "answer": "Yes – both can cause skin reactions, but for different reasons.\n\n— Latex contains natural proteins that can trigger immediate (Type I) allergies.\n\n— Nitrile is latex-free, but most nitrile gloves contain rubber accelerators (such as thiurams, dithiocarbamates, and diphenylguanidine). These chemicals are used to manufacture the gloves and are a common cause of delayed allergic contact dermatitis (Type IV) — leading to irritated skin.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq_cR9VYK": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will this heal my skin issues?",
            "answer": "DRYE doesn’t treat skin conditions. It helps remove the physical trigger many glove-wearers deal with every day: sweat trapped against the skin.\n\nWhen your hands sit wet inside sealed gloves, the skin can feel waterlogged, soft, itchy, cracked or irritated. DRYE adds a thin dry layer under your glove so sweat moves away from the skin-side while you work.\n\nMany customers tell us their hands feel better within the first day or first few weeks.",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
      },
